#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133523 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16e43d27-ffaf-58ea-bb6e-664b70770003" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Span of Two Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "c430a11a-8595-568f-9768-aad8a8de415f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5581" :question/problem "Consider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $\\mathcal B$ of $V,$ given below. Given that $\\mathcal B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\dfrac{a}{c}?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070290/assets/8f4bdd28c709b7f1b162e98ad196547f90c577df74e52fb6eef1d88efa209af9.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070290/assets/948d731d51fff2a1462db9eacab511eefbfdb1634bc9c45b2cf92ca67c4a4d18.png)\n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n- **Step 1**: We set ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070290/assets/e8a3c7b01b547745c01f123634ca06ffc966ef9acec682af33f05066e00ceef1.png)\n\n- **Step 2**: Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_2$, using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf v_2$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 1+5\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{3} \\\\ \\frac{10}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070290/assets/bb718fb5d9931c6f99268f40bbef401b50dc160304b271bd2035528bddabdec0.png) is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=-\\dfrac 7 3,$ $c=\\dfrac{10}{3}$ and  \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{c} = \\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{10}{3}\\right)} = -\\dfrac{7}{10}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133524 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b15719b6-0ce4-5fb8-80be-abcaf9344a10" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Column Space of a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "47fadc87-f265-5f1c-827a-28e5be9e8121" :question/math-academy-id "e-5582" :question/problem "Consider the subspace $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $\\mathcal B$ of $\\textrm{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $\\mathcal B$ are given below. Given that $\\mathcal B$ is derived from $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\dfrac{c}{a}?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070290/assets/9af89cb1c6f57895919cfec30701a6aa0da76adafcae91a0636b1e7ee3a89e1a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070290/assets/fc5495a909acfddca53e63d336f5d7c2261ee6df626f9db2ab9fe4f9714a3198.png)\n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n- **Step 1**: We set ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070290/assets/8db4c83e14f998397313580b16662b9c80e44bda34da21e8bf92b4a4e1d89ab8.png)\n\n- **Step 2**: Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_2$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf v_2$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-5)\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-2)+(-3)\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070290/assets/061b35426271be433b6f2a92dd3e189edfd3a725dd0fac17cb865a4984c0911d.png) is an orthogonal basis for $\\textrm{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=-4,$ $c=-1,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{c}{a} = \\dfrac{-1}{-4} = \\dfrac{1}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133525 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ccdefb8e-1bb5-52e9-812d-d2a5bffff731" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthonormal Basis of a Vector Space"}] :question/id #uuid "60dbc829-01fc-5283-8741-68af483d5313" :question/math-academy-id "e-5583" :question/problem "Consider the subspace $V$ and an **orthonormal** basis $\\mathcal B$ of $V,$ given below. Given that $\\mathcal B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|a|+|b|+|c|?$\n'\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070290/assets/9c34e0953d430b0a657c7a784e1929a874479e507a1fa1082ceff8d4f3db6340.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070290/assets/07343af18e216ada95c688294d28b29fb1e87e34e7e3485b1bf742295fcd2a44.png)\n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n- **Step 1**: We set ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070290/assets/7d5937987c8cbcab12bbb0f1349e330d221163ec557e7ae2aecee98b3cad072c.png)\n\n- **Step 2**: Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_2$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf v_2$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{6\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070290/assets/61267a1a5ef9c57217e148bccbbfa84095d5e838c968ba2cb626c6ef8baa4f58.png)\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070290/assets/fa5773aa1c638263efeb922d8c605068e0ec30070b01ee89c2c902f1977c81b8.png)\n\nTherefore, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070290/assets/990c25b329f93c269df9291bf8d6521bd7ff7e474f46815f95749abfb843fe83.png) is an orthonormal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=\\dfrac{2}{3},$ $b=-\\dfrac1  3,$ $c=\\dfrac{2}{3},$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c| = \\left| \\dfrac{2}{3} \\right| + \\left|-\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right| + \\left| \\dfrac{2}{3} \\right| = \\dfrac 5 3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365738 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b15719b6-0ce4-5fb8-80be-abcaf9344a10" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Column Space of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81d42f5b-fd76-5aa6-b07f-544427f84625" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365740} {:answer/id #uuid "4c8f4c7c-bfc9-5d87-9bb4-13097d502c5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365741} {:answer/id #uuid "12448f8a-21f3-5b30-9e0d-d2b1095a08f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365742} {:answer/id #uuid "1d4c9991-800e-5591-a757-dcef0c9ef565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365743} {:answer/id #uuid "81ccdecd-a14b-55f4-b058-51d14fc50966" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12448f8a-21f3-5b30-9e0d-d2b1095a08f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365742} :answer-field/id #uuid "d624331f-0183-5c1a-a539-731051a03962" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365739}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "124cd860-ae59-51c1-ad02-1a179d680c9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-140529" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$ \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 3}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $b=2,$ $c=-\\frac{1}{2},$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{2}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}=-4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365745 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ccdefb8e-1bb5-52e9-812d-d2a5bffff731" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthonormal Basis of a Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe97330d-27e0-5494-8f26-111b5713b378" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365747} {:answer/id #uuid "708121fe-18ed-5ede-825b-d4b0074b5ba1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365748} {:answer/id #uuid "573e0830-6a21-592b-8555-4531ee3244bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365749} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d06a7d-abf2-581a-98b5-eddc9247248e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365750} {:answer/id #uuid "ff2cdffe-5a1d-5397-a3ab-de1de3ac50b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365751}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0d06a7d-abf2-581a-98b5-eddc9247248e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365750} :answer-field/id #uuid "20cdb0d8-83e5-5e81-83ab-aa4c6c50de0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365746}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6876e598-e4f1-547d-a2d9-0f26842bf21e" :question/math-academy-id "q-140537" :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]},\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|a|+|b|+|c|?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, $B={{u}_{1},{u}_{2}}={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}]}$ is an  orthonormal basis of $V.$   \n\nFinally, $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6},$ $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3},$ $c=-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}$ and   \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}|+|\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}\\,|+|-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}|=\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365752 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ccdefb8e-1bb5-52e9-812d-d2a5bffff731" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthonormal Basis of a Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1e6fb0d-26c7-5c34-a694-38776d0f4a5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365754} {:answer/id #uuid "3cb3faac-8c3a-55bd-a7fa-80a31e9f40d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365755} {:answer/id #uuid "8b090b44-24fe-5c33-851c-ae6d677f8836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365756} {:answer/id #uuid "4471dd05-2f63-5ae6-a6f3-d3d72b3ac3f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365757} {:answer/id #uuid "4f46f6b7-52ef-5793-b5e9-2e15aaa4c15b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365758}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b090b44-24fe-5c33-851c-ae6d677f8836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365756} :answer-field/id #uuid "8552b761-5c83-5ad2-82ca-75234de75e84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365753}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "94688131-b2c5-511f-94cd-d923b0e6c74e" :question/math-academy-id "q-140539" :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]},\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|a|+|b|+|c|?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$  \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$  \n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{0\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-1)+(-3)\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, $B={{u}_{1},{u}_{2}}={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}]}$ is an  orthonormal basis of $V.$   \n\nFinally, $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},$ $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},$ $c=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}|+|\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}|+|-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}|=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134234 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16e43d27-ffaf-58ea-bb6e-664b70770003" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Span of Two Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e18e9cb-53d3-54c0-8fa5-12ed87e0978e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131847} {:answer/id #uuid "df20fa7e-5d6b-5709-9c33-aaf5e5a5bc79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131848} {:answer/id #uuid "e1288fa5-7fb0-5538-a980-97abd0a0c13a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131849} {:answer/id #uuid "26860e90-83f5-51a0-8bea-9ee7c9ea797b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131850} {:answer/id #uuid "60fd8365-c59e-555d-94b4-148530f59ae1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df20fa7e-5d6b-5709-9c33-aaf5e5a5bc79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131848} :answer-field/id #uuid "1350956a-27b1-5ed2-9254-fd3cfeaed2c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6cd93368-184b-51ef-8337-6d58bffaad14" :question/math-academy-id "q-40738" :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]},\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-3)+0\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=2,$ $c=-1,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{2}{-1}=-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134237 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b15719b6-0ce4-5fb8-80be-abcaf9344a10" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Column Space of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d768c5a1-073a-59ba-96f6-a74c4401996f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" 