{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 3+1\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 0}{{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=\\frac{1}{2},$ $b=\\frac{3}{2},$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365759 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b15719b6-0ce4-5fb8-80be-abcaf9344a10" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Column Space of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "219181f0-2150-5926-8409-34c3ece83125" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365761} {:answer/id #uuid "eb19c697-2e04-5812-a140-d2a422b0c91a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365762} {:answer/id #uuid "0bd30a8f-9b8d-5d95-aae1-0f261dcf8875" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365763} {:answer/id #uuid "1fe994a4-036d-5d3a-be52-3eb5f2c6cd85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365764} {:answer/id #uuid "d20709ae-a847-51de-8e0a-8b009b573ca6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365765}] :answer-field/correct {